## DESCRIPTION
## Calculus Two, Sequences
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Infinite sequences and series)
## DBsection(Limits of Sequences)
## Date(09/10/2018)
## Institution(Community College of Denver, Colorado Community College System)
## Author(Brittni Lorton)
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'sequences', 'find terms of a sequence')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "PGcourse.pl",);
Context('Numeric')->variables->are(n => 'Real');

$k  = non_zero_random(-10, 10);
$b  = non_zero_random(-10, 10);
$an = Formula("$b+$k*(-1)^n")->reduce;

@a = map { $an->eval(n => $_) } (1 .. 6);

BEGIN_PGML

Find the first six terms of the following sequence.

[``a_n=[$an]``], for [`n\geq 1`].

[`a_1=`][_________]{$a[0]}

[`a_2=`][_________]{$a[1]}

[`a_3=`][_________]{$a[2]}

[`a_4=`][_________]{$a[3]}

[`a_5=`][_________]{$a[4]}

[`a_6=`][_________]{$a[5]}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For this, just substitute in the value of [`n`] into the formula.

[`a_1 = [$k](-1)^1 [@ $b > 0 ? '+' : '-'@][@ abs($b) == 1 ? '': abs($b) @] = [$a[0]] `]

[`a_2 = [$k](-1)^1 [@ $b > 0 ? '+' : '-'@][@ abs($b) == 1 ? '': abs($b) @] = [$a[1]] `]

[`a_3 = [$k](-1)^1 [@ $b > 0 ? '+' : '-'@][@ abs($b) == 1 ? '': abs($b) @] = [$a[2]] `]

[`a_4 = [$k](-1)^1 [@ $b > 0 ? '+' : '-'@][@ abs($b) == 1 ? '': abs($b) @] = [$a[3]] `] 

[`a_5 = [$k](-1)^1 [@ $b > 0 ? '+' : '-'@][@ abs($b) == 1 ? '': abs($b) @] = [$a[4]] `] 

[`a_6 = [$k](-1)^1 [@ $b > 0 ? '+' : '-'@][@ abs($b) == 1 ? '': abs($b) @] = [$a[5]] `] 

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
